<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#pound-sterling">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pound sterling</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sterling</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The pound sterling is the currency of the United Kingdom. In the nineteenth century, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a>'s financial power, it served as the world's principal reserve currency. Its dominance underpinned the global trade and investment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pound-sterling.jpg" alt="Pound sterling" width="330" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abxbay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:One_Pound_1983_obverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…extensive economic system the world had yet seen, linking the agricultural resources of India, the plantation economies of the Caribbean and later West Africa and Malaya, the mineral wealth of Australia and South Africa, and the manufacturing output of industrial Britain within a <b>sterling</b>-based trading and monetary system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Yandabo of February 1826 forced King Bagyidaw to cede Arakan and Tenasserim, renounce claims to Assam and Manipur and pay an indemnity of one million pounds <b>sterling</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the repeal of the Corn Laws in 1846 the country embraced free trade, and London became the centre of world finance, shipping and insurance, with <b>sterling</b> on the gold standard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In what is now Kansas City, Missouri, Union troops under Samuel R. Curtis decisively defeated the outnumbered Confederates of <b>Sterling</b> Price, with more than 30,000 men engaged.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After their defeat at Westport on 23 October, <b>Sterling</b> Price's Confederate raiders withdrew into Kansas; Union troops then defeated them at Marais des Cygnes in Linn County, at Mine Creek and at the Marmiton River.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-25">25 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Iuka, Mississippi, Union Brigadier General William S. Rosecrans stopped the advance of the Confederate Army of the West under Major General <b>Sterling</b> Price.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-19">19 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
